1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,402
Numbers in tables are not one type.

2
00:00:02,402 --> 00:00:03,763
Integers count.

3
00:00:03,763 --> 00:00:07,416
Decimals measure money and other exact fractions.

4
00:00:07,416 --> 00:00:10,194
Floats approximate scientific values.

5
00:00:10,194 --> 00:00:12,666
If you mix them, you will round a dollar.

6
00:00:12,666 --> 00:00:17,513
INTEGER, INT, BIGINT are whole numbers of different ranges.

7
00:00:17,513 --> 00:00:20,375
customer_id is an integer.

8
00:00:20,375 --> 00:00:23,430
quantity is an integer if you sell whole books.

9
00:00:23,430 --> 00:00:25,472
Do not use a float for a count.

10
00:00:25,472 --> 00:00:29,722
DECIMAL p comma s stores exact decimal digits.

11
00:00:29,722 --> 00:00:31,402
p is total digits.

12
00:00:31,402 --> 00:00:33,472
s is digits after the point.

13
00:00:33,472 --> 00:00:36,916
DECIMAL ten comma two is a common money shape.

14
00:00:36,916 --> 00:00:40,361
Forty-two dollars and fifteen cents is exact.

15
00:00:40,361 --> 00:00:44,027
NUMERIC is often the same family as DECIMAL.

16
00:00:44,027 --> 00:00:47,694
REAL and DOUBLE PRECISION are binary floating point.

17
00:00:47,694 --> 00:00:52,125
They are fast, and they cannot represent one tenth exactly.

18
00:00:52,125 --> 00:00:54,680
Summing a column of floats can drift.

19
00:00:54,680 --> 00:00:57,250
That drift is unacceptable for money.

20
00:00:57,250 --> 00:00:59,500
It may be fine for a sensor reading.

21
00:00:59,500 --> 00:01:02,194
SQLite has a softer type system.

22
00:01:02,194 --> 00:01:06,763
A column declared INTEGER can still hold text unless you constrain it.

23
00:01:06,763 --> 00:01:08,736
Affinity is not a CHECK.

24
00:01:08,736 --> 00:01:13,638
If you need integer only, say so with a constraint or a stricter engine.

25
00:01:13,638 --> 00:01:17,541
Auto incrementing integers make convenient surrogates.

26
00:01:17,541 --> 00:01:18,930
They are not magic.

27
00:01:18,930 --> 00:01:21,583
Gaps appear after a rolled-back insert.

28
00:01:21,583 --> 00:01:23,847
Do not treat them as row counts.

29
00:01:23,847 --> 00:01:28,277
BOOLEAN is a number in some dialects and a real type in others.

30
00:01:28,277 --> 00:01:31,277
Store it as a real boolean when you can.

31
00:01:31,277 --> 00:01:34,458
Or as a constrained integer zero or one.

32
00:01:34,458 --> 00:01:37,430
Do not store yes as text if you mean a flag.

33
00:01:37,430 --> 00:01:39,555
Overflow is a real error.

34
00:01:39,555 --> 00:01:41,569
TINYINT cannot hold a million.

35
00:01:41,569 --> 00:01:45,736
Pick a range with room, not a range that matches today's sample.

36
00:01:45,736 --> 00:01:47,111
Write the sentence.

37
00:01:47,111 --> 00:01:53,375
This column is a count of books, whole, never negative, fits in a normal integer.

38
00:01:53,375 --> 00:01:58,208
This column is a price in dollars, exact cents, never a float.

39
00:01:58,208 --> 00:02:00,125
Then the type is obvious.

40
00:02:00,125 --> 00:02:03,208
Money in integer cents is a valid design.

41
00:02:03,208 --> 00:02:07,569
total_cents INTEGER, and you format dollars at the edge.

42
00:02:07,569 --> 00:02:10,222
DECIMAL is the other valid design.

43
00:02:10,222 --> 00:02:14,458
Mixing them in one table is a third, invalid design.

44
00:02:14,458 --> 00:02:17,083
Pick one and convert at the boundary.

45
00:02:17,083 --> 00:02:19,444
Percentages can be DECIMAL too.

46
00:02:19,444 --> 00:02:23,000
A float percent will surprise you in a tax calculation.

47
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:28,194
Scientific measurements can stay FLOAT if the instrument was already approximate.

48
00:02:28,194 --> 00:02:31,166
Do not fake precision the sensor never had.

49
00:02:31,166 --> 00:02:34,930
SERIAL and IDENTITY are integer generators.

50
00:02:34,930 --> 00:02:36,694
They are not a type of quantity.

51
00:02:36,694 --> 00:02:38,375
Do not use them on total.

52
00:02:38,375 --> 00:02:41,375
Unsigned integers exist in some engines.

53
00:02:41,375 --> 00:02:45,208
They forbid negatives, which a CHECK can also do more clearly.

54
00:02:45,208 --> 00:02:49,194
Prefer a signed integer plus CHECK if you want portable SQL.

55
00:02:49,194 --> 00:02:52,222
Division in integer types truncates.

56
00:02:52,222 --> 00:02:56,597
quantity divided by two may be a whole number you did not mean.

57
00:02:56,597 --> 00:03:00,722
If you need a rate, use DECIMAL and say how many places.

58
00:03:00,722 --> 00:03:03,875
NaN and infinity can appear in floats.

59
00:03:03,875 --> 00:03:05,861
They should never appear in money.

60
00:03:05,861 --> 00:03:09,597
The family you chose at the start forbids them, or should.

61
00:03:09,597 --> 00:03:18,263
Write examples in the comment: quantity 2, total 42.00, tax_rate 0.08.

62
00:03:18,263 --> 00:03:22,611
If an example needs a float, you picked the float family on purpose.

63
00:03:22,611 --> 00:03:24,861
Counts, money, science.

64
00:03:24,861 --> 00:03:26,013
Three homes.

65
00:03:26,013 --> 00:03:27,777
Put each column in one.

66
00:03:27,777 --> 00:03:31,916
If a spreadsheet formatted it as currency, it is not a float.

67
00:03:31,916 --> 00:03:34,472
If a sensor emitted it, it might be.

68
00:03:34,472 --> 00:03:37,430
If a person counted it, it is an integer.

69
00:03:37,430 --> 00:03:40,750
Say those three sentences when a column is born.

70
00:03:40,750 --> 00:03:44,916
Then DECIMAL, FLOAT, or INTEGER is not a debate.

71
00:03:44,916 --> 00:03:46,569
It is a classification.

72
00:03:46,569 --> 00:03:49,597
Misclassification is how pennies vanish.

73
00:03:49,597 --> 00:03:54,041
Pennies vanishing is how finance stops trusting the database.

74
00:03:54,041 --> 00:03:56,527
Keep their trust with exact types.

75
00:03:56,527 --> 00:04:00,972
Keep science honest with approximate types that admit they are approximate.

76
00:04:00,972 --> 00:04:02,263
Keep counts whole.

77
00:04:02,263 --> 00:04:04,236
The rest is dialect spelling.

78
00:04:04,236 --> 00:04:10,180
Whole numbers, approximate fractions, and exact decimals are three different jobs.

79
00:04:10,180 --> 00:04:18,069
Integers count things: quantities, ids you generated, flags stored as zero and one if you must.

80
00:04:18,069 --> 00:04:20,486
Signed integers allow negatives.

81
00:04:20,486 --> 00:04:23,847
Unsigned, where the engine has them, do not.

82
00:04:23,847 --> 00:04:28,194
TINYINT through BIGINT are range costumes around the same idea.

83
00:04:28,194 --> 00:04:30,180
Do not memorize the ranges.

84
00:04:30,180 --> 00:04:36,888
Look them up when a column is born, and pick the smallest range that cannot overflow in real life.

85
00:04:36,888 --> 00:04:42,583
Overflow is a silent disaster if the engine wraps, or a loud one if it errors.

86
00:04:42,583 --> 00:04:43,722
Prefer loud.

87
00:04:43,722 --> 00:04:47,069
FLOAT and DOUBLE are binary approximations.

88
00:04:47,186 --> 00:04:53,149
They are correct for scientific measurements where 0.1 repeating is a known compromise.

89
00:04:53,149 --> 00:04:54,719
They are wrong for money.

90
00:04:54,719 --> 00:05:02,816
The Vancouver stock index story is the parable: tiny errors, added every trade, became a crash in trust.

91
00:05:02,816 --> 00:05:08,261
If you add a price to a price in FLOAT, you can get a value that is not any real cent.

92
00:05:08,261 --> 00:05:15,663
DECIMAL, also called NUMERIC, stores exact base-ten values with a declared precision and scale.

93
00:05:15,663 --> 00:05:23,024
DECIMAL eight comma two means eight digits total, two after the point: a money column with room to breathe.

94
00:05:23,024 --> 00:05:29,011
Money, interest rates you must audit, and anything a receipt will print belong in DECIMAL.

95
00:05:29,011 --> 00:05:33,774
Percentages you will multiply back into money belong in DECIMAL too.

96
00:05:33,774 --> 00:05:40,941
Sensor readings, graphics, and statistics that already live in floating point may honestly be FLOAT.

97
00:05:40,941 --> 00:05:43,483
Say approximate in the comment if you do.

98
00:05:43,483 --> 00:05:46,899
Do not use FLOAT because it felt like the default number.

99
00:05:46,899 --> 00:05:48,622
There is no default number.

100
00:05:48,622 --> 00:05:54,302
There is a classification: counted, exact fraction, approximate fraction.

101
00:05:54,302 --> 00:05:58,441
If a spreadsheet formatted it as currency, it is not a float.

102
00:05:58,441 --> 00:06:01,399
If a person counted it, it is an integer.

103
00:06:01,399 --> 00:06:04,761
If a lab instrument emitted it, it might be a float.

104
00:06:04,761 --> 00:06:07,788
Misclassification is how pennies vanish.

105
00:06:07,788 --> 00:06:12,233
Pennies vanishing is how finance stops trusting the database.

106
00:06:12,233 --> 00:06:14,719
Keep their trust with exact types.

107
00:06:14,719 --> 00:06:18,358
Keep science honest with types that admit they are approximate.

108
00:06:18,358 --> 00:06:19,649
Keep counts whole.

109
00:06:19,649 --> 00:06:26,288
The rest is spelling in your dialect: INT versus INTEGER, DECIMAL versus NUMERIC.

110
00:06:26,288 --> 00:06:27,816
Spelling is easy.

111
00:06:27,816 --> 00:06:30,122
Classification is the lesson.

112
00:06:30,122 --> 00:06:33,761
Close with questions, the way a careful lecture does.

113
00:06:33,761 --> 00:06:36,816
Answer in a sentence you could defend at a keyboard.

114
00:06:36,816 --> 00:06:38,052
Integers for?

115
00:06:38,052 --> 00:06:39,816
Counts and whole ids.

116
00:06:39,816 --> 00:06:41,038
DECIMAL for?

117
00:06:41,038 --> 00:06:43,288
Money and exact fractions.

118
00:06:43,288 --> 00:06:44,316
FLOAT for?

119
00:06:44,316 --> 00:06:47,080
Approximate measurements that admit error.

120
00:06:47,080 --> 00:06:48,497
Why not FLOAT money?

121
00:06:48,497 --> 00:06:50,719
Binary error accumulates.

122
00:06:50,719 --> 00:06:52,274
Stock index parable?

123
00:06:52,274 --> 00:06:54,649
Tiny errors, lost trust.

124
00:06:54,649 --> 00:06:56,636
Classification sentences?

125
00:06:56,636 --> 00:06:59,205
Counted, exact, approximate.

126
00:06:59,205 --> 00:07:03,927
If an answer needed a second table, go draw it before you write more SQL.

